Fix: CLI-tokenized task gets tokenized twice when deferred for a free slot

Base commit: 9a3bf2b84923a85583b4ee8177b0cca13824bb03 (gguf-v0.19.0-1114-g9a3bf2b84)

Root cause: process_single_task() tokenizes task.cli_prompt via
tokenize_cli_input() and never resets task.cli back to false. If no slot is
free at that moment, the (already-tokenized) task is moved wholesale into
queue_tasks_deferred and later re-dequeued into process_single_task() again
once a slot frees up. Since task.cli is still true, tokenize_cli_input() runs
a second time -- but task.cli_prompt was already cleared by the first run, so
the second tokenization produces empty tokens, which the server then treats
as an "empty prompt" request and returns an empty response with
stop_type=none.

This only affects the CLI-tokenization path (task.cli = true), used by
in-process embedders like mineru_llama_cpp's EngineCore that call
server_context directly without going through the HTTP layer. The normal
HTTP path tokenizes prompts synchronously in the request-handling thread
before the task ever reaches the queue, so it never sets task.cli and is
unaffected -- queuing/deferring behaves correctly there.

Symptom: with N concurrent requests > n_parallel slots, the requests that
have to wait for a slot to free up return empty content instead of their
generated text.

diff --git a/tools/server/server-context.cpp b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
index 744593c76..3b0c20a7f 100644
--- a/tools/server/server-context.cpp
+++ b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
@@ -2225,6 +2225,9 @@ private:
             }
             task.cli_prompt.clear();
             task.cli_files.clear();
+            task.cli = false; // prevent re-tokenizing an already-cleared cli_prompt if this
+                               // task gets deferred (no free slot) and later re-dequeued --
+                               // process_single_task() re-checks task.cli on every dequeue
         } catch (const std::exception & e) {
             send_error(task, std::string("Failed to format input: ") + e.what(), ERROR_TYPE_INVALID_REQUEST);
             return false;
